When a small group of living things (a small population) departs from the principal they come from (e.g., if they move to a mountain range or a river, or if they move to a new island so that they can’t recede easily) they will often find themselves in an environment other than they used to.
The new environment has different resources and competitors and, therefore, a new population will need different characteristics or adaptations than it had before to be a strong competitor.
There have been no changes in the original population, which still need the same adaptations as in the past.
Over time, as the fledgling population adapts to the new environment, it begins to resemble less and less to the other population.
Finally, after thousands or even millions of years, the two populations will look so different that it will not be possible to categorize them as of the same species.
This process is called speciation, which simply signifies the development of new species, and is an inescapable consequence and a fundamental part of evolution.
