The history of Taiwan dates from the 15th century, when the sailors of Europe who were navigating the place registered the name of the island as Ilha Formosa, that is, beautiful island.
The establishment, in 1624, by the Dutch East India Company, of a base in southwestern Taiwanese, initiated a transformation in the aboriginal method of grain production, with the use of Chinese labor to work on its rice and sugar plantations.
The Qing Dynasty Army (1644–1912) seized control of Taiwan's Nordic and western coastal region in 1683 and declared the latter a Qing Empire-dependent province in 1885.
In 1895, after having been defeated during the First Sino-Japanese War, (1894-1895), the Qing government adheres to the Shimonoseki treaty, through which Taiwan's sovereignty cedes to Japan, which retains its control over the island until 1945.
