Of the seven wonders, the only one still standing is the Great Pyramid of Giza.
The Great Pyramid, erected by the Egyptian people in the 3rd century B.C., is one of the many pyramid-shaped and oversized structures built to revere the deceased pharaoh.
The Giza Plateau, or “Necropolis of Giza”, in the Valley of the Dead in Egypt, contains numerous pyramids (of which the Great Pyramid is the largest), several small tombs, temples, and the great Sphinx.
The Great Pyramid was built in honor of Pharaoh Khufu, and many others of this type, tombs and smaller temples were erected in honor of their wives and relatives.
