The High Middle Ages was preceded by the Early Middle Ages and was followed by the Late Middle Ages which, by convention, ends about 1500.
Technological determinism is a concept that pragmatically understands a wide range of ideas, from technological impulse or imperative to a strict sense that there is an underlying logic that drives the fate of humanity and that relates to the laws of science and how they are embodied in technology.
Almost all interpretations of technological determinism have in common two general ideas: that the technological development itself unfolds along a path independent of any cultural or political influence and that, at the same time, technology has “effects” on societies that are inherent and not socially conditioned.
To give an example, one could argue that the existence of automobiles necessarily drifts into the development of roads.
However, a national road network is not economically viable for a reduced volume of cars, thereby designing new manufacturing techniques to reduce the costs of owning a vehicle.
Massive use of cars also generates a higher incidence in traffic accidents, which leads to the development of new techniques in the field of health to repair damaged bodies.
The element of cultural determinism was very present in romanticism, according to scholars such as Goether, Fichte and Schlegel.
In the context of Romanticism, geography shaped people and, over time, customs and cultures developed related to that geography that, being in harmony with the location of that society, were preferable to laws that were arbitrarily imposed.
Just as Paris is known as the city of fashion today, Constantinople was regarded as such in feudal Europe.
His reputation as an epicenter of luxury began in approximately 400 B.C. and stretched up to about 1100 A.D.
During the 12th century their status decayed, above all because, on their return, the Crusaders brought gifts, such as silks and spices, which were given greater value than the goods offered in the Byzantine markets.
The title of "fashion capital" was transferred, thereafter, from Constantinople to Paris.
The cusp of the gothic style took place in the period between the 10th and 14th centuries.
