Even though project-based learning should facilitate that process and make it more interesting, scaffolding goes a little further.
Scaffolding is not a learning technique; it is, rather, an assistance that provides support to those who are going through a new experience, such as the use of a new computer program or the start of a new project.
Scaffolding can be presented in both real and virtual format; to put it another way, a teacher is a type of scaffolding, but so is the small animated clipboard of Microsoft Office.
Virtual scaffolds are built into the software and their function is to question, stimulate or explain procedures that may have been complex for the student alone.
