The stirrups are the foot supports of the riders hanging on both sides of the saddle.
While they provide greater stability to the rider, they can present safety issues, as it is possible for their feet to get stuck in them.
If a rider is fired from the horse, but has one foot stuck in the stirrup, it can be dragged if the animal flees. To reduce the risk, there are a number of safety collections.
First of all, most riders wear taco riding boots that have a smart and fairly narrow sole.
In addition, there are frames, especially English ones, with safety bars that allow stirrup leather to detach from its structure if a fired rider pulls backwards.
