For the Christian religion, Christmas is one of its most important festivities, celebrating the birth of Jesus Christ.
Numerious traditions related to this festivity have also been incorporated by non-believers from both Christian and non-Christian countries around the globe.
It is tradition to spend the Easter night somewhere exposed to contemplate the sunrise.
There are, of course, Christian theological explanations of this tradition, but it may well be a pre-Christian ritual of spring and fertility.
More traditional churches usually offer a Passover Vigil on Saturday night during the Easter weekend and, in general, the congregations begin celebrations for the resurrection of Christ as soon as midnight arrives.
All animals that originally came to the islands came to this place swimming, flying or floating.
Because they are far removed from the mainland, the mammals failed to get there, which caused the giant turtle to become the main grazing creature in the Galapagos.
Many mammalian species have been introduced to the Galapagos since man arrived in the archipelago, such as, for example, goat, equine and beef cattle, or rodents, dogs and felines.
